declines over rounds, suggesting semantic compression.\nL264: The results reveal that LLM groups converge lexically and semantically, develop asymmetric influence patterns, and exhibit negotiation-like behaviors despite the absence of explicit role prompting. This work demonstrates how black-box interaction analysis can surface emergent coordination strategies, offering a scalable complement to internal probe-based interpretability methods.\nL265:     %R 10.18653/v1/2025.blackboxnlp-1.12\nL266:     %U https://aclanthology.org/2025.blackboxnlp-1.12/\nL267:     %U https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.blackboxnlp-1.12\nL268:     %P 206-225\nL269: Download as File\nL270: ##### Markdown (Informal)\nL271: \nL272: [Emergent Convergence in Multi-Agent LLM Annotation](https://aclanthology.org/2025.blackboxnlp-1.12/) (Parfenova et al., BlackboxNLP 2025)\nL273: \nL274:   * cite37†Emergent Convergence in Multi-Agent LLM Annotation (Parfenova et al., BlackboxNLP 2025)\nL275: ##### ACL\nL276: \nL277:   * Angelina Parfenova, Alexander Denzler, and Jürgen Pfeffer. 2025. cite37†Emergent Convergence in Multi-Agent LLM Annotation . In Proceedings of the 8th BlackboxNLP Workshop: Analyzing and Interpreting Neural Networks for NLP, pages 206–225, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL278: ACL materials are Copyright © 1963–2026 ACL; other materials are copyrighted by their respective copyright holders. Materials prior to 2016 here are licensed under the cite39†Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 International License†creativecommons.org . Permission is granted to make copies for the purposes of teaching and research. Materials published in or after 2016 are licensed on a cite40†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License†creativecommons.org .\nL279: The ACL Anthology is managed and built by the cite5†ACL Anthology team of volunteers.\nL280: \nL281: Site last built on 12 October 2026 at 19:21 UTC with cite41†commit a75fc